# Database Design & ORM Standards

You are a database-first engineer. The database is the foundation of every application. A poorly designed schema creates compounding technical debt that is expensive to fix later.

---

## 1. Schema Design Principles

### Naming Conventions

```prisma
// ✅ Prisma Schema — Correct Naming
model User {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())       // Use cuid() or uuid() — never auto-increment for public IDs
  email     String   @unique
  name      String
  role      Role     @default(VIEWER)
  isActive  Boolean  @default(true)
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())             // Always include timestamps
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt                   // Auto-updated on every change

  posts     Post[]
  profile   Profile?

  @@map("users")                                  // Map to snake_case table name
}

model Post {
  id          String   @id @default(cuid())
  title       String   @db.VarChar(200)           // Explicit length constraints
  slug        String   @unique @db.VarChar(250)
  content     String   @db.Text
  excerpt     String?  @db.VarChar(500)
  status      PostStatus @default(DRAFT)
  publishedAt DateTime?
  viewCount   Int      @default(0)
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime @updatedAt

  authorId    String
  author      User     @relation(fields: [authorId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  categories  Category[]
  tags        Tag[]

  @@index([authorId, createdAt(sort: Desc)])      // Composite: "posts by user, newest first"
  @@index([status, publishedAt(sort: Desc)])      // Composite: "published posts, newest first"
  @@index([slug])                                  // Unique lookup by slug
  @@map("posts")
}

enum Role {
  ADMIN
  EDITOR
  VIEWER
}

enum PostStatus {
  DRAFT
  PUBLISHED
  ARCHIVED
}
```

**Naming Rules:**
| Element | Convention | Example |
|---------|-----------|---------|
| Models (Prisma) | PascalCase singular | `User`, `Post`, `OrderItem` |
| Tables (SQL) | snake_case plural | `users`, `posts`, `order_items` |
| Columns | snake_case | `created_at`, `is_active`, `full_name` |
| Enums (Prisma) | PascalCase | `Role`, `PostStatus` |
| Enum values | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `ADMIN`, `DRAFT`, `IN_PROGRESS` |
| Foreign keys | `{relation}Id` | `authorId`, `categoryId` |
| Indexes | Named by purpose | `@@index([authorId, createdAt])` |

### Data Type Selection

```prisma
// ✅ Choose correct types
model Product {
  id          String   @id @default(cuid())
  name        String   @db.VarChar(200)         // Bounded strings
  description String?  @db.Text                 // Unbounded text
  price       Decimal  @db.Decimal(10, 2)       // NEVER use Float for money
  quantity    Int      @default(0)
  weight      Float?                             // Float OK for measurements
  isActive    Boolean  @default(true)
  metadata    Json?                              // Flexible key-value data
  tags        String[]                           // PostgreSQL array type
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime @updatedAt
}
```

**Type Rules:**
- **Money / Currency:** Always use `Decimal(10,2)` — never `Float` (floating-point precision errors).
- **IDs:** Use `cuid()` or `uuid()` for public-facing IDs. Auto-increment integers leak information (record count, creation order).
- **Strings:** Set explicit `@db.VarChar(n)` for bounded fields (names, titles, slugs). Use `@db.Text` for unbounded content (articles, comments).
- **Timestamps:** Every table must have `createdAt` and `updatedAt`.
- **Booleans:** Prefix with `is`/`has`/`can` (`isActive`, `hasVerifiedEmail`, `canPublish`).
- **Nullable:** Default to required (`String`). Only use optional (`String?`) when null genuinely means "no value".

