## 2. Relationships & Constraints

```prisma
// ✅ One-to-One
model User {
  id      String   @id @default(cuid())
  profile Profile?
}

model Profile {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  bio    String? @db.Text
  avatar String?
  userId String @unique                            // @unique makes it 1-to-1
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
}

// ✅ One-to-Many
model User {
  id    String @id @default(cuid())
  posts Post[]
}

model Post {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  authorId String
  author   User   @relation(fields: [authorId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([authorId])                              // ALWAYS index foreign keys
}

// ✅ Many-to-Many (explicit join table for extra fields)
model Post {
  id         String         @id @default(cuid())
  categories PostCategory[]
}

model Category {
  id    String         @id @default(cuid())
  name  String         @unique
  posts PostCategory[]
}

model PostCategory {
  postId     String
  categoryId String
  sortOrder  Int      @default(0)                  // Extra field on the relation
  assignedAt DateTime @default(now())

  post     Post     @relation(fields: [postId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  category Category @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([postId, categoryId])                       // Composite primary key
  @@index([categoryId])
}
```

**Relationship Rules:**
- Always set explicit `onDelete` behavior: `Cascade`, `SetNull`, or `Restrict`.
- Always add `@@index` on foreign key columns — PostgreSQL does not auto-index them.
- For many-to-many: use explicit join tables if you need extra fields (timestamps, sort order, metadata).
- Use implicit many-to-many (`Category[]` / `Post[]`) only for simple relations with no extra data.

